		(uuid "6452bc6c-ae82-418a-88f8-3e16ef74e284")
	)
	(text_box "What voltages are these? VDD/VCC/VDDIO are all for your reference. You need to actually describe what voltage (3V3 or 5V) is going into these ICs for a proper review to happen. - Joel\nRemember that in breakout boards, it clarifies this with 5V input pin for example. You can use the data sheet to figure this out. - Sarah"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 49.53 13.9343 0)
		(size 46.228 21.1177)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "07ce5577-53aa-482a-9f88-f603a5f356b4")
	)
	(text_box "Why are the I2C lines just tied to voltage? How are you getting the signal away from the BMM?\nAre you trying to use pull-up resistors or line impedence resistors here?\nI suggest you look into I2C works before re-working this schematic.\nSame with the INT pin, the image has the VDDIO pull up as DNP (Do Not Place), so how are you getting the signal out?\n- Joel\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 206.756 46.482 0)
		(size 43.18 25.146)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "16e1daca-434d-4524-a0ba-4549371062fc")
	)
	(text_box "This isn't wrong, but try to stay consistent accross the *entire* team of using international or US symbols for comoponents. International Caps will just have 2 bars for non-electrolytic caps and a curved bar for electrolytic caps.\nI recommend using the international for the Caps and US for resistors. \"C\" and \"R_US\" respectively.\n- Joel"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 36.576 45.212 0)
		(size 35.56 28.4838)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "34aa9633-e55f-42d2-bd38-408f3366993b")
	)
	(text_box "These are pull-up resistors. On a PCB this will be close to a uC. - Joel"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 90.932 127.8146 0)
		(size 18.288 14.1714)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "9e8fddd8-2716-4464-9bf6-d3305137d82f")
	)
	(text_box "<- These pins allows for higher level schematics (like the PCB) to be able to input things into this schematic. You'll see this in Digital Logic more but for now, you can press \"H\", give it a name and then connect schematic specific names afterwards and power flags - Sarah"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 51.308 97.028 0)
		(size 33.274 22.35)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
